    "narrative": "The premier play for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, boasting an elite 85.94 score against Rhett Lowder. The combination of an favorable matchup (4.72 xERA) and Lowder\u2019s slow delivery creates a high-upside environment for **Corbin Carroll** to exploit his elite sprint speed for stolen bases. **Mickey Moniak** also projects as a high-tier start at 75.98; he benefits significantly from a 14% boost due to park factors and wind conditions, alongside a strong platoon advantage against Tanner Bibee. **Gabriel Moreno** rounds out our top-tier trio (70.08), capitalizing on the favorable matchup against Lowder and a prime slot in the three-hole, providing stability at the C1 position.\n\nThe lineup optimization reflects a heavy emphasis on platoon and park advantages, most notably with **Travis Bazzana** (61.43). Despite his uncertain lineup placement, the synergy of a wind-aided environment and a solid matchup against Hughes gives **Travis Bazzana** a clear edge over the remainder of the bench. We are wary of the floor for **Max Muncy** (50.43) and **Zach McKinstry** (51.25), both of whom are pushed into service due to roster constraints and matchup variables. **Cole Young** was the most difficult exclusion from the starting nine; his 44.13 score was weighed down heavily by his placement in the eight-hole and a less-than-ideal matchup against Peterson, ultimately leaving him on the bench despite his stolen base potential.\n\nManagers should keep a close eye on the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Max Muncy** and **Travis Bazzana**, as significant drops in their batting order could necessitate a pivot to late-swaps to preserve efficiency. While **Isaac Paredes** (57.15) and **Josh Naylor** (57.62) provide mid-range security, the primary risk remains the offensive inconsistency of the bottom half of the order. With **James Wood** sidelined on the IL, we remain thin on high-ceiling bench depth, making it vital that **Corbin Carroll** and 